    <h1>The Present</h1>
    <h2 id="newera">The New Era</h2>
    <h3>The Modern League</h3>
    <figure>
        <img src="images/I20N_WRC.jpg" />
        <figcaption>Hyundai i20N Rally1</figcaption>
    </figure>

     <p>In 2017, the WRC underwent one of its most dramatic rule changes in decades, introducing wider, faster, and more aerodynamically aggressive cars than anything the sport had seen in years.
        The new regulations brought increased downforce, improved mechanical grip, and raw power outputs that made these machines spectacular to watch on every surface. Stages that once took minutes
        longer were now completed at breathtaking speeds, raising the bar for both drivers and engineers.</p>
     <p>    
         Major manufacturers including Toyota Gazoo Racing, Hyundai Motorsport, and M-Sport Ford
        threw themselves into the new formula, producing an era of fierce and closely fought competition. Then in 2022, the championship took another leap forward with the introduction of
        hybrid-powered Rally1 cars, blending cutting-edge electrification with the raw demands of rally racing. But through it all, one team has stood above the rest:
    </p>

    <h2 id="toyota">TOYOTA</h2>
    <h3>The Most Dominant Team</h3>
    <figure>
        <img src="images/YARIS_WRC.jpg"/>
        <figcaption>Toyota GR Yaris Rally1</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Toyota Gazoo Racing has been the defining force of the modern WRC era. Returning to the championship in 2017 with the Yaris WRC, the Japanese manufacturer wasted no time establishing
        itself at the front of the field. Their transition to the hybrid-powered GR Yaris Rally1 in 2022 only tightened their grip on the sport. Engineered to the highest standards and
        </p>
    <p>
        constantly developed throughout the season, the GR Yaris Rally1 combines extraordinary pace on all surfaces with the reliability needed to win championships. Driven by a roster of
        elite talent, Toyota have claimed five manufacturers' titles in the modern era — in 2018, 2019, 2021, 2022, and 2023 — making them the most successful manufacturer in the history
        of the reformed championship, and cementing the GR Yaris Rally1 as one of the most formidable rally cars ever built.
    </p>
