    <h2 id="icons">The Icons</h2>
    <h3>Definers Of This Age</h3>
    <figure>
        <img src="images/rovanpera.jpg" />
        <figcaption>Kalle Rovanperä</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>The modern era of the WRC has produced some of the most talented and celebrated drivers the sport has ever seen. Unlike their predecessors, today's rally drivers must master hybrid systems
        and ever-evolving car setups in addition to the traditional demands of reading stages and managing pace notes at extreme speed. The championship has grown more competitive than ever, with
        margins between the top teams often measured in seconds across an entire season.</p>
    <figure>
        <img src="images/ogier.jpg" />
        <figcaption>Sébastien Ogier</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Sébastien Ogier, a nine-time world champion, has continued to defy time with part-season campaigns that still regularly end in victories. Kalle Rovanperä became the youngest WRC champion
        in history in 2022 at just 22 years old, and immediately backed it up with a second consecutive title in 2023. Ott Tänak, the 2019 champion, has remained a constant threat across
        multiple manufacturers, while Thierry Neuville finally claimed his long-awaited title in 2024 for Hyundai after years of near misses. Together, these drivers have made the present era
        of the WRC one of the most exciting and unpredictable in the championship's history.</p>
